  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single metropolitan area, and the specific performance thresholds of the models may differ in other urban housing markets.",
      "what": "The study evaluated the spatial accuracy of five automated valuation models—ordinary least squares, geographically weighted regression, random forest, extreme gradient boosting, and multilayer perceptron—across housing submarkets in Greater Sydney. Random forest achieved the highest overall predictive accuracy, while geographically weighted regression performed competitively only in stable submarkets characterized by homogeneous housing and high transaction volumes. Correlation analysis showed that model accuracy systematically improves in areas with lower average housing prices, lower price dispersion, and higher sales volumes.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that automated valuation model performance is highly sensitive to local market conditions, showing that practitioners cannot rely on a single universal model and should instead tailor model selection to specific submarket characteristics."
    }
